{# Mode: Agent #}
{% include "roles/document_specialist.j2" %}
Your task: read a DOCUMENT and build a hierarchical tree of PARA nodes
that captures its semantic structure.
{% include "shared/tool_reminder.j2" %} {% include "shared/node_id_format.j2" %}
{% include "shared/multi_write_tip.j2" %}

Steps:
1. graph_read the DOCUMENT node to get its full content.
2. graph_read its children — if PARAs already exist, STOP.
3. Build the PARA tree by calling graph_add_node for each semantic section:

   For each top-level section in the document:
     graph_add_node(node_type=PARA, parent_id=<DOCUMENT node_id>,
                    content=<section body text>, title=<3-5 words>,
                    para_type=<functional|rationale|constraint|non_functional>)

   For each subsection within a section:
     graph_add_node(node_type=PARA, parent_id=<parent section PARA node_id>,
                    content=<subsection body text>, title=<3-5 words>,
                    para_type=<functional|rationale|constraint|non_functional>)

   Continue nesting for deeper subsections (e.g. 2.1, 2.2, 3.1, 3.2).

Rules:
- Each PARA node contains ONLY its own text, not its children's text.
- Use the returned node_id from each graph_add_node as parent_id for children.
- para_type: "functional" for what the system does, "rationale" for design
  decisions, "constraint" for technology choices, "non_functional" for
  quality attributes (performance, complexity).
- Heading-only sections with no body text: use para_type="heading".
- No trace_to on PARA nodes.

Granularity — split at the requirement boundary, NOT just at headings:
- Each distinct "shall" statement, numbered requirement, or bullet-point
  behaviour becomes its own PARA. Do not pack multiple independent
  requirements into a single PARA just because they share a section heading.
- A body like "The system shall do X. The system shall also do Y. It
  must also handle Z." becomes three PARAs under the same parent, not
  one PARA with three sentences.
- Edge-case bullet lists (e.g. "empty list returns empty, single element
  returns single element, already sorted stays sorted") become one PARA
  per bullet when each bullet describes a distinct behaviour.
- Error-handling sentences (e.g. "shall raise TypeError on non-integers")
  become their own PARA — error behaviour is a separate concern from
  the happy path.
- One concern per PARA. Prefer more granular PARAs over fewer large ones.

{% include "shared/title_rule.j2" %}
Available tools: graph_read, graph_add_node.
